Teach grpcio's setup.py that iOS and Android exist, and stop it handing the
cross compiler the build host's /usr/include.

grpcio compiles the whole gRPC C core plus its vendored third-party trees
(c-ares, re2, upb, abseil, zlib, boringssl) into a single Cython extension,
and setup.py selects the include directories for those trees from
`sys.platform`. Under forge the cross Python reports the TARGET platform --
"ios" or "android" -- which upstream's chain of `if "<os>" in sys.platform`
tests knows nothing about, so every branch falls through. meta.yaml also asks
the Android leg to build against a system OpenSSL and zlib
(GRPC_PYTHON_BUILD_SYSTEM_OPENSSL / _ZLIB = 1) instead of the vendored
boringssl/zlib, and upstream implements "system" as the literal path /usr --
right on a Linux builder, but on our macOS build host that is the *build*
machine's headers.

Three hunks, all in setup.py:

  1. c-ares config header. The vendored c-ares needs a generated
     `ares_config.h`, and setup.py adds exactly one
     `third_party/cares/config_<os>` directory to the include path based on
     sys.platform. "ios" matches nothing, and although the sdist does ship
     `third_party/cares/config_android`, upstream never wired up a branch
     that selects it. Add both: iOS piggybacks on config_darwin (Apple libc,
     same answers), Android gets config_android. Without this the c-ares
     sources compile with no ares_config.h to find.

  2. System-OpenSSL include path. With GRPC_PYTHON_BUILD_SYSTEM_OPENSSL set,
     the boringssl sources are filtered out of the build and SSL_INCLUDE is
     hardcoded to /usr/include/openssl. Read the prefix from OPENSSL_ROOT_DIR
     instead -- meta.yaml points it at `{platlib}/opt`, where the openssl
     wheel unpacks inside the cross environment -- and use `<prefix>/include`,
     the parent of `openssl/`, which is what grpc's `#include <openssl/...>`
     lines actually need. The default stays "/usr" when the variable is
     unset, so nothing changes for a target that isn't asking for system
     OpenSSL.

  3. zlib include path. Same story: GRPC_PYTHON_BUILD_SYSTEM_ZLIB drops the
     vendored zlib in favour of `-lz` and sets ZLIB_INCLUDE to the host's
     /usr/include. Rather than special-case the value, this hunk lifts
     SSL_INCLUDE and ZLIB_INCLUDE out of the EXTENSION_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES
     tuple literal and appends them afterwards, skipping ZLIB_INCLUDE
     entirely on Android. Android's zlib headers come from the NDK sysroot
     and `-lz` resolves there too, so nothing is lost -- whereas
     `-I/usr/include` on a build host with the Command Line Tools installed
     offers the cross compiler a directory full of macOS headers. Moving
     SSL_INCLUDE to the end of the list is incidental; the directories are
     disjoint, so search order does not matter here.

Hunks 2 and 3 only bite on Android. iOS builds the vendored boringssl and
zlib, so its SSL_INCLUDE/ZLIB_INCLUDE still point inside third_party/ and are
untouched by either change; hunk 1 is the one that matters there.

Provenance note: neither the commit that introduced this patch (the
repo-consolidation squash, #64) nor the PR it came from (#12, "Grpcio for iOS
and Android", empty body) records a rationale. The explanation above is read
off the diff, grpcio 1.81.0's setup.py, and the matching keys in meta.yaml's
script_env -- it is reconstruction, not a quote from the author. One further
piece of history worth knowing: between #65 and the 1.67.1 -> 1.81.0 bump
(#68) this patch also carried a fourth hunk relaxing the `fdopen` stub in
third_party/zlib/zutil.h on Apple platforms. The bump removed it and the
builds stayed green; why it was needed, and why it stopped being needed, is
not recorded either.

--- a/setup.py	2026-03-31 12:55:12
+++ b/setup.py	2026-03-31 12:56:03
@@ -58,12 +58,14 @@
     os.path.join("third_party", "cares"),
     os.path.join("third_party", "cares", "cares"),
 )
-if "darwin" in sys.platform:
+if "darwin" in sys.platform or "ios" in sys.platform:
     CARES_INCLUDE += (os.path.join("third_party", "cares", "config_darwin"),)
 if "freebsd" in sys.platform:
     CARES_INCLUDE += (os.path.join("third_party", "cares", "config_freebsd"),)
 if "linux" in sys.platform:
     CARES_INCLUDE += (os.path.join("third_party", "cares", "config_linux"),)
+if "android" in sys.platform:
+    CARES_INCLUDE += (os.path.join("third_party", "cares", "config_android"),)
 if "openbsd" in sys.platform:
     CARES_INCLUDE += (os.path.join("third_party", "cares", "config_openbsd"),)
 RE2_INCLUDE = (os.path.join("third_party", "re2"),)
@@ -302,7 +304,9 @@
         lambda x: "third_party/boringssl" not in x, CORE_C_FILES
     )
     CORE_C_FILES = filter(lambda x: "src/boringssl" not in x, CORE_C_FILES)
-    SSL_INCLUDE = (os.path.join("/usr", "include", "openssl"),)
+    # Use cross-compiled OpenSSL headers when OPENSSL_ROOT_DIR is set
+    _ssl_prefix = os.environ.get("OPENSSL_ROOT_DIR", "/usr")
+    SSL_INCLUDE = (os.path.join(_ssl_prefix, "include"),)
 
 if BUILD_WITH_SYSTEM_ZLIB:
     CORE_C_FILES = filter(lambda x: "third_party/zlib" not in x, CORE_C_FILES)
@@ -329,14 +333,17 @@
     + ADDRESS_SORTING_INCLUDE
     + CARES_INCLUDE
     + RE2_INCLUDE
-    + SSL_INCLUDE
     + UPB_INCLUDE
     + UPB_GRPC_GENERATED_INCLUDE
     + UPBDEFS_GRPC_GENERATED_INCLUDE
     + UTF8_RANGE_INCLUDE
     + XXHASH_INCLUDE
-    + ZLIB_INCLUDE
 )
+
+# On Android, SSL_INCLUDE uses OPENSSL_ROOT_DIR; skip ZLIB_INCLUDE to avoid /usr/include
+EXTENSION_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES += SSL_INCLUDE
+if "android" not in sys.platform:
+    EXTENSION_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES += ZLIB_INCLUDE
 
 EXTENSION_LIBRARIES = ()
 if "linux" in sys.platform:
